This builds the list's mail hub on trunk. It turns the relay jail
into the list's home in steps, one service at a time. Each step
leaves a jail that works and does what its README.org says.

How mail moves, end to end, once the series is applied:

  client --SMTP--> OpenSMTPD --LMTP--> Dovecot --> INBOX
  Dovecot <--IMAP--> mbsync <--IMAPS--> Fastmail
    (INBOX, Next, Later, and Archive only)

New mail lands in INBOX, whether it is posted to lab-mail or arrives
at Fastmail. lab-mail syncs exactly four folders with Fastmail: INBOX,
Next, Later, and Archive. Any other folder Pat makes on Fastmail
stays there, and reaches neither lab-mail nor any notmuch. There is no
lists folder, no Sieve, and no Fastmail rule. Dovecot is the only
program that writes the maildir. Every reader may read every folder,
and only Pat and mbsync may change anything.

The folders come from Fastmail. Dovecot creates none, and mbsync
creates on lab-mail whichever of the four it finds on Fastmail. It
never creates one on Fastmail.

- Patch 1 is "[PATCH v2] jails/instances/lab-mail: Spec the list's
  mail hub" [1], unchanged. You found it ready to implement [2], but
  it is not on trunk, and the rest of the series edits the spec it
  adds, so the series carries it.
- Patch 2 corrects the spec on what Dovecot does to a reader's write.
  A STORE or EXPUNGE answers OK and changes nothing. Only append,
  copy, and move are refused.
- Patch 3 narrows the spec's header check to what OpenSMTPD does on
  its own.
- Patch 4 changes the spec to keep the list in INBOX of the list's
  own account, and to sync INBOX and Archive and no other folder,
  with the folders made on Fastmail only. The Fastmail rule, the
  lists folder, and the moderation queue go.
- Patch 5 makes Dovecot the only writer of the list. mbsync fetches
  Fastmail's INBOX one way into Dovecot's INBOX over IMAP, as the
  login mbsync. This is the one step that needs the maildir and
  mbsync's state emptied first.
- Patch 6 turns the fetch into a two-way sync of INBOX and Archive.
  mbsync creates Archive on lab-mail from Fastmail's.
- Patch 7 adds Pat's login, so he can archive and delete on lab-mail.
- Patch 8 swaps smtprelay for OpenSMTPD, which hands posts to Dovecot
  over LMTP, into INBOX. smtprelay, jq, and the mailcred group go
  with it.
- Patch 9 documents the one-time import of the jj maildir into INBOX.
- Patch 10 adds lab@lists.p7x.dev and freebsd-ports@lists.p7x.dev to
  the addresses OpenSMTPD delivers to the list.
- Patch 11 syncs Next and Later beside INBOX and Archive, and specs
  the client side: the folder is a message's only state, clients
  derive their tags from it, and Pat's notmuch syncs flags.

Before deploying: delete the Fastmail rule that files list mail into
lists, and create Next and Later on Fastmail. Mail still in lists on
Fastmail stays there, outside the list, until you move it to INBOX or
Archive.

I applied the series with git am onto a fresh export of trunk, and
each libexec script passes sh -n. Dovecot, smtpd, and the jail build
are not installed here, so no step was run on a jail. In particular,
that the ACL's k right lets mbsync create a top-level mailbox under
acl_globals_only is untested.

Changes in v12:
- Base the series on trunk instead of b/dev, so it applies to trunk.
  v11 sat on the spec patch [1] as b/dev carried it, and b/dev no
  longer does, so the spec patch is now patch 1.
- The other patches are unchanged, and the lab-mail tree at the end
  of the series is identical to v11's. There is no interdiff, since
  one against v11 would show only the change of base.
